.TH UPTIME 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
uptime \- show how long the system has been running
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B uptime
.RB [ \-s ]
.br
.B uptime \-\-help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B uptime
writes one line to standard output: the current local time, the time
that has passed since Windows started, and the load averages.
With
.BR \-s ,
it writes instead the date and time at which Windows started.
It takes a single sample and exits.
.PP
The default line has these parts, in order:
.IP \(bu 2
A space and the current local time as
.IR HH : MM : SS
on a 24-hour clock.
.IP \(bu 2
.B up
and the time since Windows started.
If that is a day or more, it starts with the number of whole days,
unpadded, a space, the word
.B day
or
.BR days ,
a comma, and a space.
Then, if the hours left over are not zero, come the hours, padded on
the left with a blank to 2 characters, a colon, and the minutes as 2
digits.
Otherwise come the minutes, 0 to 59 and unpadded, a space, and
.BR min ,
as in
.BR "7 min" .
Seconds are not shown.
.IP \(bu 2
A comma, two spaces, and
.B load average:
followed by three figures with two decimal places, separated by a comma
and a space.
The figures are always
.BR 0.00 ,
because this port computes no load average.
.PP
No count of users is printed.
For example, after one day, 5 hours, and 42 minutes the line is
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
 21:26:06 up 1 day,  5:42,  load average: 0.00, 0.00, 0.00
.EE
.RE
.PP
With
.BR \-s ,
the output is the start time as
.IR YYYY \- MM \- DD
.IR HH : MM : SS
in local time, with no leading space and nothing else.
.SH OPTIONS
Operands are ignored.
Options may follow operands unless
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
is set.
.B \-\-
ends the options.
Apart from
.BR \-\-help ,
there are no long options.
.TP
.B \-s
Print the time at which Windows started, as described above, instead of
the usual line.
It is computed as the current time less the time since Windows started
in whole seconds, so two runs can differ by a second, and a change to
the clock since Windows started changes the result.
.TP
.B \-\-help
As the first argument, print a usage message on standard output and
exit with status 0.
Later arguments are ignored.
The message does not mention
.BR \-s .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TZ
The local time zone, for the current time and for
.BR \-s .
If it is unset or empty, the Windows time zone setting applies.
The format is that of the Microsoft C runtime, such as
.B EST5EDT
or
.BR JST\-9 ;
it is described in
.BR date (1).
A zone database name is misread: with
.BR Europe/Paris ,
the zone is
.B Eur
at UTC, with daylight saving time named
.B ope
on the United States rules.
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
If set, the first operand ends the options, so that a later
.B \-s
is an operand and is ignored.
.PP
The variables that busybox reads for every applet, such as
.BR BB_GLOBBING ,
are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, or
.B \-\-help
was given.
.TP
.B 1
An unknown option.
A message naming the option, and then a usage message, are written to
standard error.
.PP
An error writing standard output is not detected, and the status is 0.
.SH EXAMPLES
Show the time, the uptime, and the load:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ uptime
 21:26:06 up 1 day,  5:42,  load average: 0.00, 0.00, 0.00
.EE
.RE
.PP
Show when Windows started:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ uptime \-s
2026-10-18 15:43:41
.EE
.RE
.PP
Convert the start time to seconds since the epoch:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ date \-d "$(uptime \-s)" +%s
.EE
.RE
.PP
Test in a script whether Windows has been running for a day or more:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ uptime | grep \-q day && echo up for a day or more
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
The time since Windows started is
.B GetTickCount64
divided by 1000, dropping the fraction.
It does not come from
.IR /proc/uptime .
On Windows XP, which lacks
.BR GetTickCount64 ,
it is
.BR GetTickCount ,
which returns to zero every 49.7 days, so a longer uptime is shown
short by a multiple of that period and
.B \-s
is wrong by the same amount.
.PP
The count includes time spent in sleep and hibernation.
With fast startup, which is on by default in Windows 8 and later,
shutting down and starting again does not reset it; a restart does.
.PP
Differences from the procps
.B uptime
on Linux:
.IP \(bu 2
The only option is
.BR \-s .
There is no
.BR \-p ,
.BR \-h ,
.BR \-V ,
or long option other than
.BR \-\-help .
Operands are ignored.
.IP \(bu 2
The load averages are always 0.00.
.IP \(bu 2
The number of users, which busybox prints when it is built with
.I utmp
support, is never printed, because this build has none.
.PP
The line ends with LF, not CRLF.
The output is the same on a console and when redirected, and has no
escape sequences.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR date (1),
.BR free (1),
.BR ps (1)
